    "scenario": "MPLS TE Fast Reroute Link Protection",
    "goals": "• OSPF area 0, addressing, Loopback0 interfaces, an MPLS core with LDP and the MPLS traffic engineering baseline are already configured on all five core links.\n• {{router1_name}} already has the primary TE tunnel Tunnel{{primary_tunnel_id}} towards {{router4_name}} Loopback0 ({{router4_loopback0_ip}}), pinned through {{router2_name}} by the explicit path {{primary_path_name}}, announced into OSPF and up.\n• If the {{router1_name}} <-> {{router2_name}} link {{link1_cidr}} fails, the primary tunnel is torn down and has to be resignalled end to end. Protect it locally instead:\n    build a next-hop backup tunnel Tunnel{{backup_tunnel_id}} on {{router1_name}} whose destination is {{router2_name}} Loopback0 ({{router2_loopback0_ip}})\n    the backup tunnel must avoid the protected link and reach {{router2_name}} through {{router3_name}}, listing the hops {{link3_ip2}} and {{link5_ip1}} in an explicit path named {{backup_path_name}}\n    the backup tunnel must not reserve any bandwidth\n• Bind the backup tunnel to the protected interface on {{router1_name}}, and mark the primary tunnel as eligible for fast reroute so it actually uses the backup on failure.\n• Both tunnels must be up, the backup must transit {{router3_name}}, and the primary must still transit {{router2_name}}.",
